                    round_name_r4:          `Prove`,

                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Scope the real challenge`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Nordpharm's sponsor describes the problem three different ways: <em>“managers don't decide fast enough,” “we have a cross-site collaboration gap,” “first-line leadership is weak.”</em> These are not the same problem, and a program scoped to all three measures as a failure no matter how well delivered. Converge on <strong>one sharply defined behavior change</strong> the program will attack — expressed as something you can measure at work, like <strong>“ops managers resolve cross-site quality escalations within 48 hours”</strong> — name the success metric, and decide what to explicitly leave out of scope. Scope precedes design: a program scoped to a fog cannot prove transfer.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Select the action-learning format`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Design the learning model against the <strong>€4,500</strong> ceiling. High-touch action-learning on the real Nordpharm challenge drives transfer but runs ~€6,200/learner alone; a scalable digital cohort hits ~€2,800 but historically delivers the 22% transfer you must beat. Blend live project teams, coached sponsor reviews, in-person intensives across 6 countries, and digital modules — engineered so learners <strong>work on the Round-1 challenge</strong>, the action-learning core — at or below €4,500. Then drive completion <em>without padding</em>: relevance and an accountable sponsor finish a program; mandated seat-hours do not.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Embed the AI tool and lock the plan`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Decide how far to embed the AI personalization engine. Adaptive pacing and AI coaching prompts <strong>enhance how learners work the real challenge</strong>; a skills-diagnostic that profiles every learner is powerful but the works-council flashpoint. <em>Mid-round event:</em> the works council files a formal data-privacy query under GDPR, and the vendor reveals the diagnostic adds <strong>€320/learner</strong>. Design the data and consent approach — what is collected, who sees it, what is opt-in — and a fallback if the diagnostic is blocked. AI in L&D is a governance problem, not a feature; and it enhances action-learning, it does not replace it.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Prove the impact`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Present the locked design to the CHRO and a works-council representative. Report against the four KPIs — <strong>transfer-to-job, skill gain, completion and cost per learner</strong> — with an explicit measurement design: how you will evidence on-the-job behavior change (manager/sponsor ratings at 90 days, movement on your Round-1 business metric), <em>not just a happy sheet</em>. State your target transfer rate versus the 22% baseline and defend it with the design you built. Then make the one-paragraph case for why this design renews the €3.6M framework — and remember: a program learners love is not the same as one that changes what they do.`,

                    r1_challenge_label:     `The sponsor gives three conflicting problem statements. Converge on ONE challenge to attack.`,
                    r1_metric_label:        `Define the success metric the program will be judged on.`,
                    r1_scope_label:         `How disciplined is your out-of-scope list — what the program will explicitly NOT do? (%)`,
                    r1_scope_readout:       `Out-of-scope discipline:`,
                    r1_pct:                 `%`,
                    r1_scope_hint:          `A program that tries to do everything proves nothing. Excluding the wrong problems is how the right behavior gets the design time and the measurement.`,
                    ch_behavior:            `One sharp behavior — “ops managers resolve cross-site quality escalations within 48 hours” — tied to a business metric`,
                    ch_decisions:           `“Managers decide faster” — pick the speed-of-decision framing because it sounds the most measurable`,
                    ch_vague:               `Keep it broad — “strengthen first-line leadership” so the program covers all three concerns the sponsor raised`,
                    ch_all:                 `Attack all three at once — collaboration, decision speed and leadership — so we don't miss what the board cares about`,
                    met_business:           `Movement on the scoped business metric — mean cross-site escalation resolution time at the 9 sites`,
                    met_knowledge:          `End-of-program knowledge test pass rate`,
                    met_satisfaction:       `Average satisfaction score (the 4.3/5 we already know how to move)`,

                    r2_alloc_label:         `Blend the format against the €4,500 ceiling. Allocate per-learner spend across the menu (€).`,
                    r2_total_readout:       `Per-learner cost:`,
                    r2_total_cap:           `/ €4,500 ceiling`,
                    r2_completion_label:    `How do you drive completion without padding the program?`,
                    alloc_action:           `Live action-learning project teams on the real Nordpharm challenge (highest transfer, ~€6,200/learner at full intensity)`,
                    alloc_sponsor:          `Coached sponsor reviews — managers' bosses on the hook for results (drives transfer, sponsor-time cost)`,
                    alloc_intensive:        `In-person intensives across the 6 countries (high impact, heavy travel cost)`,
                    alloc_digital:          `Peer-cohort digital modules (scalable, ~€2,800/learner alone, historically weak transfer)`,
                    comp_sponsor:           `Sponsor accountability + visible progress on their own real project — completion follows relevance`,
                    comp_grades:            `Grade and rank learners to pressure them through`,
                    comp_hours:             `Mandate seat-hours — require everyone to log the full module time`,

                    r3_embed_label:         `How far do you embed the AI personalization engine?`,
                    r3_consent_label:       `Design the data & consent approach for the works council and GDPR.`,
                    r3_fallback_label:      `Set a fallback if the works council blocks the AI diagnostic mid-launch.`,
                    r3_event_hint:          `Mid-round event: the works council files a formal data-privacy query, and the vendor reveals the skills-diagnostic adds €320/learner. Re-balance scope, format and AI spend without breaching €4,500.`,
                    ai_enhance:             `Adaptive pacing + AI coaching prompts between sessions — personalize HOW people work the real challenge, no profiling`,
                    ai_diagnostic:          `Full deployment incl. the skills-diagnostic that profiles every learner — the most powerful, most visible option`,
                    ai_replace:             `Lean on the AI to carry the program and thin the live project work to save cost — let personalization do the heavy lifting`,
                    ai_off:                 `Keep it off this cohort — the engine is unproven and the board only “wants it visible”; don't risk the launch`,
                    cons_minimized:         `Data minimization + explicit opt-in, learner sees their own profile, no manager-level profiling, human oversight`,
                    cons_notice:            `Privacy notice only — tell learners their data is processed, no opt-in, manager dashboards on by default`,
                    cons_none:              `Switch it on — it's a learning tool; we'll handle privacy questions if they come up`,
                    fb_manual:              `Manual self-assessment + facilitator triage replaces the diagnostic; the action-learning core is unaffected`,
                    fb_pause:               `Pause the whole launch until the diagnostic is cleared — the AI was the differentiator after all`,
                    fb_none:                `No fallback — if the diagnostic is blocked we'll improvise; it probably won't happen`,

                    r4_measure_label:       `Design how you will evidence on-the-job behavior change to the CHRO.`,
                    r4_target_label:        `Set your target transfer-to-job rate versus the 22% baseline.`,
                    r4_case_label:          `The renewal case: why does THIS design renew the €3.6M framework?`,
                    r4_conviction_label:    `Conviction: how firmly do you hold that a program that changes behavior beats one learners merely love? (%)`,
                    meas_transfer:          `Manager + sponsor behavior ratings at 90 days AND movement on the Round-1 business metric — evidence behavior, not reaction`,
                    meas_knowledge:         `Pre/post knowledge test — prove they learned the content`,
                    meas_happy:             `Post-program satisfaction survey — the happy sheet the board has already seen`,
                    tgt_credible:           `45% — a credible doubling, evidenced by the design choices you made`,
                    tgt_moonshot:           `80% — promise the board a transformational number to win the renewal`,
                    tgt_safe:               `25% — barely above baseline, safe to defend`,
                    case_evidence:          `“We will evidence behavior change on the metric you chose, with sponsors accountable” — lead with proof of transfer`,
                    case_ai:                `“We deployed cutting-edge AI personalization” — lead with the technology differentiator`,
                    case_polish:            `“It's our most polished, best-rated program ever” — lead with delivery quality`,

                    warn_scope_range:       `Out-of-scope discipline must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_alloc_negative:    `Format allocations cannot be negative.`,
                    warn_alloc_zero:        `You must allocate at least some budget to the format — a program with €0 of design buys nothing.`,
                    warn_ai_choice:         `Choose how far to embed the AI engine before locking the round.`,
                    warn_conviction_range:  `Conviction must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>That is a challenge I can measure.</strong> “Resolve cross-site escalations within 48 hours” is one behavior, at work, with a number attached. Now every downstream choice — format, AI, measurement — can aim at it. This is the discipline the last 12 programs skipped.`,
                    coach_r1_partial:       `<strong>“Decide faster” is closer, but to what?</strong> Faster than what, measured how, on which decisions? It sounds measurable but you haven't tied it to a business metric yet. Sharpen it to a behavior I can see at the 9 sites, or the design has nothing to aim at.`,
                    coach_r1_vague:         `<strong>“Strengthen leadership” is exactly the fog that produced 22% transfer.</strong> In week 17 I will ask: did managers collaborate better, decide faster, or lead more? You will have a satisfaction score and no answer. What is the <em>one</em> behavior this program changes?`,
                    coach_r1_all:           `<strong>Attacking all three problems means designing for none.</strong> Three behaviors in 16 weeks on €4,500 dilutes the action-learning core until nothing transfers. The board cares about results, not coverage — pick the one behavior that, if it moves, the others follow.`,
                    coach_r1_metricbiz:     `<strong>Scope plus a business metric — that is the whole game.</strong> One behavior, measured on escalation-resolution time at the 9 sites. Now the program can prove transfer instead of asserting it, and I have something to put in front of the board.`,
                    coach_r1_metricsat:     `<strong>A satisfaction score is the metric that already failed you.</strong> 4.3 out of 5 across 12 programs and 22% transfer — measuring reaction is precisely why the board stopped believing the happy sheet. Judge this program on behavior at work.`,
                    coach_r1_noscope:       `<strong>No out-of-scope list means scope creep is already winning.</strong> If you won't say what the program will <em>not</em> do, the three problem statements will all sneak back in and you are back to the fog. Excluding is how the right behavior gets the time.`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>That is a transfer-driving blend.</strong> A real action-learning core on the Nordpharm challenge, accountable sponsors, and just enough digital to scale — inside the ceiling. This is the design that beats 22% without blowing €4,500.`,
                    coach_r2_cheap:         `<strong>You hit budget on a digital-heavy cohort — and bought the 22%.</strong> Under €4,500, congratulations; but your last digital cohort delivered the exact transfer you must beat. You saved euros to guarantee the outcome that loses the €3.6M framework. What did the savings actually buy?`,
                    coach_r2_goldplate:     `<strong>Beautiful design — and over the ceiling.</strong> Live teams plus in-person intensives in all six countries breaches €4,500 with no contingency, so Nordpharm will not pay it. Which elements actually drive transfer, and which are expensive comfort you can cut?`,
                    coach_r2_padding:       `<strong>Mandating seat-hours is padding, not completion.</strong> Hours logged is not behavior changed; people finish a program when it is relevant to their real work and a sponsor is watching, not because you counted the minutes.`,
                    coach_r2_nocore:        `<strong>There is almost no action-learning core here.</strong> Personalization and digital optimize <em>how</em> people learn content — but with no real-project spine, there is no behavior to transfer. The content was never the problem; the lack of real work is.`,
                    coach_r2_mixed:         `<strong>A spread is defensible — but tilt it toward the core.</strong> The more weight on live action-learning and accountable sponsors versus generic digital, the more transfer you buy per euro. Make sure learners are working the Round-1 challenge, not watching modules.`,
                    coach_r3_enhance:       `<strong>That is AI as an enhancer, not an experiment.</strong> Adaptive pacing and coaching prompts personalize how each manager works the real challenge — lifting engagement, completion and transfer — without profiling employees or tripping the works council. The differentiator the board wanted, governed.`,
                    coach_r3_diaggoverned:  `<strong>The diagnostic with a real consent design can hold.</strong> Explicit opt-in, data minimization and human oversight is what lets you profile responsibly — but remember it just added €320 a head. Make sure the budget still works and that the diagnostic earns its place.`,
                    coach_r3_blocked:       `<strong>You deployed learner profiling across 240 employees in 6 countries with no consent plan.</strong> The works council just filed a formal GDPR objection and your launch is frozen. The AI was meant to be your differentiator — now it is the thing that stalled the program. What data did you actually need, and what was consented?`,
                    coach_r3_diagoverbudget: `<strong>The €320 diagnostic just pushed you over €4,500.</strong> Even governed, it has to fit the ceiling Nordpharm fixed — there is no contingency line. Re-balance the format or step the diagnostic back; a great tool you can't pay for doesn't ship.`,
                    coach_r3_replace:       `<strong>You leaned on the AI to carry the program and thinned the live work.</strong> Personalization optimizes how people learn content — it does not make a generic program transfer. You automated the part that was already fine and cut the part that drives behavior change. What is the AI actually amplifying here?`,
                    coach_r3_off:           `<strong>Switching it off is safe — and throws away your differentiator.</strong> The board explicitly wanted the AI visible, and used well it lifts completion and transfer. You don't need the risky diagnostic, but adaptive pacing and coaching prompts cost you little and were the point.`,
                    coach_r3_notice:        `<strong>A privacy notice is not consent.</strong> Telling 240 employees their data is processed, with manager dashboards on by default and no opt-in, is exactly the “algorithmic profiling” the works council flagged. Minimize the data and make it opt-in, or expect the objection.`,
                    coach_r3_noconsent:     `<strong>No data plan on a live AI tool is a launch risk you didn't price.</strong> Even adaptive pacing processes learner data; without consent and minimization the works council can stall it. Design the governance now, not after the query lands.`,
                    coach_r3_pause:         `<strong>Pausing the entire launch over the diagnostic is the tail wagging the dog.</strong> The AI was meant to enhance the program, not gate it. A manual fallback keeps the action-learning core running while you clear the privacy query — don't freeze 240 managers for one feature.`,
                    coach_r4_measure:       `<strong>That is a real transfer measurement.</strong> Manager and sponsor behavior ratings at 90 days plus movement on your Round-1 business metric evidences behavior change, not reaction. This is what the board asked for — and what you can actually defend in week 17.`,
                    coach_r4_happy:         `<strong>A satisfaction survey is the happy sheet the board already rejected.</strong> 4.3 out of 5 will not renew this. You designed a program and then chose to measure the one thing that proves nothing about on-the-job performance. Evidence behavior, on your Round-1 metric.`,
                    coach_r4_credible:      `<strong>45% is a credible doubling — and your design earns it.</strong> A scoped behavior, a transfer-driving format inside budget, governed AI and a real measurement plan make that number defensible rather than hopeful. That is the difference between a target and a wish.`,
                    coach_r4_moonshot:      `<strong>80% is a number you cannot evidence.</strong> Promising the board a transformational figure with no design or measurement to back it is over-promising — and when week 17 shows half of it, you lose more trust than a credible 45% would have built. Commit only to what the design can prove.`,
                    coach_r4_safe:          `<strong>25% barely beats the baseline — why would I renew for that?</strong> A target one point above the 22% you already deliver tells the board nothing changed. If the design is strong, claim the transfer it can actually evidence; timidity loses the framework just as surely as fantasy.`,
                    coach_r4_win:           `<strong>That is the case that renews the framework.</strong> One scoped behavior, evidenced on the metric I chose, with sponsors on the hook — proof of transfer, not polish and not a tech demo. That is a program that changes what 240 managers do at work.`,
                    coach_r4_caseai:        `<strong>Leading with “we deployed cutting-edge AI” misses what I asked for.</strong> The board wanted the AI visible, yes — but they renew on transfer-to-job, not on the tool. Lead with the behavior change you will evidence; the AI is how, not why.`,
                    coach_r4_casepolish:    `<strong>“Most polished, best-rated program ever” is the 4.3 out of 5 story.</strong> Delivery quality is table stakes; I have seen 12 polished programs deliver 22% transfer. Lead with proof that behavior changes at work, or this reads as another happy-sheet pitch.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
